**Implemented and verified.**

The feature is available through **File → Load libdvdcss library…** in the GUI. It validates the selected DLL/`.so`/`.dylib`, copies it to `<data dir>/libdvdcss/`, persists the path in `app_DvdcssPath`, and automatically reopens the current disc. *Use automatic libdvdcss detection* clears the explicit path. CLI users can set `OPTICRIP_DVDCSS` or place the library manually.

Core files: [dvdcss.py](opticrip/dvdcss.py), [discfs.py](opticrip/discfs.py), [engine.py](opticrip/engine.py), [iso9660.py](opticrip/iso9660.py), [app.py](opticrip/gui/app.py), [settings.py](opticrip/settings.py), [messages.py](opticrip/messages.py), and [test_dvdcss.py](tests/test_dvdcss.py).

**Verified**

- `python -m unittest discover -s tests` → **105 tests, OK**.
- `compileall` succeeds.
- Physical `OVER_THE_HEDGE` CSS disc with official libdvdcss 1.2.11: `MSG:9121`, **13 titles**, inspection in ~6 s.
- Final CSS save after all changes: title 9, exit `0`, 23 MB MKV, MPEG-2 video + AC-3 audio, `26.999 s`; full decode exits `0`.
- GUI load, automatic reopen, and automatic-detection reset tested against the real library/disc.
- Without a library: disc inspection still works, `MSG:9120` is emitted, and saving exits `11` with the protected-media messages.

**Remaining limits**

- Encrypted `Backup` is still refused; only title saves use decryption.
- Windows libdvdcss accepts drive letters, so CSS `.iso` files require the physical disc; unencrypted ISOs are fine.
- AACS/BD+ remain unsupported.
- CSS audio bitrate rows are omitted because VOB headers are skipped during inspection.
- The remux emits pre-existing non-monotonic DTS warnings on some DVD stream copies; decoding still completes successfully.

A high-value next step is adding an automated synthetic CSS end-to-end test so full CSS save coverage no longer depends on the physical drive.
